<h3 id="3-using-reverse-function-to-detect-palindromes" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight"><span>3) Using REVERSE() function to detect palindromes</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#3-using-reverse-function-to-detect-palindromes" aria-label="Link to 3) Using REVERSE() function to detect palindromes"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h3>
<p>A palindrome is a string that reads the same forward and backward such as <code>&quot;radar&quot;</code>.</p>
<p>You can use the <code>REVERSE()</code> function to reverse a string and then compare the reversed string with the original string to determine if it is a palindrome. For example:</p>
<p>First, <a class="" href="../postgresql-tutorial/postgresql-create-table">create a new table</a> called <code>words</code> to store the words:</p>
